    "executiveSummary": "For centuries, mainstream science treated the universe like a giant mechanical clock. Think of a pocket watch: if it stops ticking, you take it apart, clean a tiny gear, and put it back together. But a living human, an ecosystem, or a city is not a clock.\n\nIf you cut open a bird to find out how it flies, its life vanishes instantly. Life is not an assembly of independent parts. It is a dynamic dance of relationships where the whole constantly generates and repairs itself from within.\n\nCapra and Luisi synthesize biology, physics, cognitive science, and sociology into a single unified architecture. They reveal that living systems are autopoietic\u2014literally self-making networks. Consider a bustling downtown traffic rotary.\n\nNo single central conductor directs every bumper, yet smooth collective motion emerges from constant local feedback loops. In the same way, cells balance chemistry, brains create awareness, and societies create culture through ongoing loops of interaction. When stress hits, living networks adapt flexibly rather than snapping like rigid steel beams.\n\nWhen we apply this lens to everyday reality, our greatest modern problems become crystal clear. Climate collapse, financial instability, chronic illness, and corporate burnout are not isolated glitches. They are cascading ripple effects caused by trying to run living networks with rigid, linear machinery.\n\nBy adopting systems thinking, leaders, innovators, and citizens can design sustainable, closed-loop solutions that work with nature rather than fighting against it.",
